WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.389
Film Courage What do you think can be helpful
for screenwriters when they look at the science

00:00:03.389 --> 00:00:04.770
behind writing and story?

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.720
Dr. Connie Shears, Professor Crean at Chapman
University and Author: I think it’s profoundly

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:19.429
helpful for a screenwriter to recognize the
physiological processes that their audiences

00:00:19.429 --> 00:00:20.970
are experiencing.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:32.029
To have at least a minimal understanding of
the visual and the auditory information is

00:00:32.029 --> 00:00:40.579
being received the same by everybody’s eyes
and ears work the same way but once that information

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:49.000
is transduced into neural impulses in the
brain that is where the screenwriter needs

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.350
to understand that every person in there is
working from a different schema, an organized

00:00:56.350 --> 00:01:04.330
set of their experiences, building up their
own individual expectations.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:11.190
Here is where the screenwriter can capitalize
on general experiences.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:19.820
So in this day and age almost everybody has
seen a movie or has watched video clips so

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:25.190
there’s a certain amount of experience that
exists at a general level.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:34.300
What can they do in their screenwriting to
make that unique, to make that individual,

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:40.300
to make their story stand out from the expected.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:50.990
I think understanding the inner weaving of
memory and language and emotion with the psychical

00:01:50.990 --> 00:02:00.290
processes of what the audience is seeing or
hearing is key to the way we dedicate our

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:03.970
book to the success of future storytellers.

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:16.420
I think storytelling will take on really fascinating
new perspectives when screenwriters begin

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:22.700
to consider the brain processes of the audience
that they are telling the story to.

